# The kernel builds olo_gpu_pages may be loaded into: each one looked at
# and tried, as kernel/README.md says ("gpu-pages"): on that exact build,
# ttm_pool_alloc_page() is one function called from one place, with its
# arguments where the module reads them (pool in %rdi, gfp in %esi, order
# in %edx), a pool's flags where the kernel's headers say; and
# tools/dev/gpu-pages-check passed in guests running that build. The
# module's own check as it loads is of what the function does, not of
# where a build keeps its arguments: a build not listed here is left as
# it comes.
#
# A line a build: the kernel's release, its build ID (/sys/kernel/notes)
# and the build ID of its ttm module (/sys/module/ttm/notes), then the
# build ID of Olo's live patch olo_gpu_memory as it was in the kernel
# beside it when it was checked: the module is loaded with no live
# patch in the kernel, or with exactly that one and no other.
#
# `tools/dev/gpu-pages-check --qualify` says a build's words, and that
# they are *pending a person's review*: its reading of the machine code
# is a help and not a proof (a review found shapes it passes that the
# probe would misread). Before a line is added, someone reads, in that
# build: the caller's setting up of the three arguments and the callee's
# use of them at the probe's site; the function's range, its aliases and
# clones; every relocation against it, in code and in data; the pool's
# layout (`dev`, `alloc_flags`, its size); and the allocation's falling
# back on refusal, in the fill and in the restore. Who, when and what
# they read goes in the commit that adds the line.
#
# 7.3.4-300.fc45: read by hand in three reviews with Astra, 2026-11-21
# (kernel/TUNING.md, "Graphics memory in 2 MB pieces").
